tuff but  you're also doing real-time combat each  sort of game had its own combat system  and each game took place in such unique  locales we're talking about so dramatic  fairytale land Middle Eastern Arabian  Nights the African savanna and Slavic  sort of vampire mythology to this day  there's never been a game series that's  been so unique in sort of concept  mechanic and story that has been as  memorable to me  the quest for glory series and that's  why I play it all the time when I was  around six my mom brought home banjo  kazooie for the n64 and she and I played  it obsessively probably for four years  until i cooled down a little bit and  then to this day I play it once every  year she and I played together and it  was a really really important bonding  experience for me we kept a notebook we  kept track of all of Brent iltis with  answers for the the trial at the end of  the game which upon reflection is  probably not my favorite part of the  game but I loved it so much as a kid I'm  it's weird because platformers really  aren't the genre I gravitate to anymore  but banjo kazooie is when i return to  every single year try to beat my lowest  time to this day i think i've done it in  eight hours i'll keep playing until i  can get to around six hours the game i  returned to pretty much every year is  the legend of zelda majora's mask  ocarina of time always used to be my  favorite zelda but the older I get and  the more I play majora's mask the more I  appreciate how weird and how bold and  how creative it is you have three days  in this world to complete the game and  of course you can end up rewinding time  and starting things over and doing  certain quests different ways and  meeting certain people at different  spots and exploring termina all over the  course of just four major dungeons as  opposed to the usual seven or eight and  a zelda game but the thing is this game  is so side quest base and rewards you so  much for exploring and finding new masks  and exploring is weird gloomy dark  pretty depressing world that you're  rewarded almost every time you find a  new area our new character you talk to  or a new master fine even more so than  any other Zelda game it's rewarding to  just meet new people and find new places  and that's why it's a game I return to  time after time so my childhood like so  many of our childhoods was completely  defined by Disney and all their amazing  movies and lucky for us a lot of those  movies became video games I'm talking  jungle book lion king and my personal  favorite Aladdin it takes you some of  the best locations like you ain't never  had a friend like me Jeanie dream  sequence level and it's got really good  music from the movie being the youngest  of three sisters I was often not able to  play because I wasn't as good at the  game as they were and it's just kind of  like just always sectioned off to the  side the problem was like back then you  couldn't save the game you had to just  play it all the way through or leave it  running  didn't like that so they could beat the  game in one sitting and I couldn't now I  can beat the game in one sitting as long  as I'm practicing and doing a good job  and that's rad that's why I want to come  back to it because I can beat this game  I can take on Jafar and I can totally  take them out with some apples to the  face and if you can take down Jafar  you're treated to a really nice  rendition of I can show you the world  and who doesn't want that I've been  playing video games for almost 30 years  and I replay a lot of my favorites but  none more so than final fantasy tactics  I love the gameplay first and foremost  it's a very difficult strategy game but  you can also break it in a lot of ways  there's a lot of you know opportunities  to be creative with different classes  and just create different teams and just  kind of infinite replay value but I also  love it because it came at a time where  you know I played Final Fantasy 6 and 7  which had more modern settings than past  games which were set in like a medieval  world tactics brought that back and it  was also really great because it was  super tragic characters died all the  time and it's just a really impactful  story even you know I played it probably  seven times over now and it still gets  me every time my magic eight day of the  destroyer is a game I play every couple  of years I'd say even though it's a huge  RPG that takes tens of hours to play  it's a turn-based RPG where you have a  party you look out from a first-person  perspective and it's not the kind of  game you really see any more there are  something so delightful about playing  this brutally hard classic western RPG  you have a huge variety in the number of  classes you can choose there are dark  elves and necromancy sand dragons that  you can opt in and out of your party you  can tailor make it for exactly the way  that you want to play I remember playing  this game as a kid and my dad had played  it before me he printed out hundreds of  pages worth of manuals because there are  so much you need to reference you need  to figure out what kind of pie you're  going to build who the classes are what  can they upgrade until what level can  they upgrade where are the teachers for  the skills that you need for each of  your different classes it's incredibly  complex and in a way it's the kind of  complex that turns people off but for me  it's just super exciting and I love  getting back into the challenge every  couple of years  those are our picks but let me know down  in that comment section which game from  your childhood you like to revisit every  so often and why and we'll catch again  next time  [Music]  you  [Music]

